# Custom reports

A custom report shows the data that you choose, in the way that you choose. You
make it from a question, such as "Is there a dawn rise?", and then you change it.
Nocturne keeps the report, so you and the other members of your account can open
it again.

To open your custom reports, select **Reports** in the app navigation, then
**Custom reports**. To make a report, select **New**.

## Start a report

The new report page starts with a list of questions under four headings:
**Glucose**, **Lows and highs**, **Meals and insulin** and **Body and devices**.
Each question has a line that tells you what its chart shows. Select
**More questions** under a heading to see all its questions.

- Select a question to start from it. The report opens with a chart that
  answers the question. You can change everything in it.
- Select **Blank report** to start with one empty chart.
- Under **Ready-made reports**, select a report to open it in the form. See
  [The form](#the-form).

## Build a chart

A chart has four parts: the signals, the lens, the groups and the options.
Above the chart, one sentence (the caption) says what the chart shows. Select a
part of the caption to go to its setting.

### Signals

A signal is one kind of data, such as glucose, insulin on board, carbs or heart
rate. The list at the side of the chart shows the signals in families:
**Glucose**, **Insulin**, **Food**, **Devices**, **Settings**, **Body** and
**Context**.

- Select a signal to put it on the chart. Select it again to remove it.
- **In other views** lists the signals that another lens can show. Select one to
  change to that lens and put the signal on the chart.
- **Not in your access** lists the signals that your access does not include.
  Each one shows the data that it needs.

Each signal on the chart has a chip above the chart. Select a chip to change how
the signal is drawn, for example as a line, an area or bars. You can also smooth
the signal, add a trend line, or give the signal a lane of its own.

### Lenses

The lens sets how time is shown:

| Lens | What it shows |
|------|---------------|
| **Timeline** | Each value at the time it occurred. |
| **Typical day** | All the days put on top of one day, as the median and the middle half. |
| **Around an event** | The hours before and after each event, put on top of each other. |
| **Day by day** | One value for each day. |
| **One against another** | Two signals as points, each 15 minutes, one along each axis. |

A lens that cannot show the chart now is not available. The reason shows under
the lens buttons.

### Groups of events

With the lens **Around an event**, you compare groups of events. Each group has a
letter: A, B, C and so on.

- Select the kind of event: for example, meals, correction boluses, lows,
  highs, exercise, sleep starts, sensor starts or site changes.
- Select **Narrow** to keep only some events of the group. You can narrow by
  time of day, weekday, meal size, food, or the time since the last carbs. You
  can also narrow by the glucose or the insulin on board at the event.
- Select **Compare with another group** to add a group.
- Select **Compare with everything else** to add a group of all the other events
  of the same kind.

A chart shows up to three groups on top of each other. From four groups, the
chart shows a small chart for each group, with the same scale on each.

**Options** sets the time before and after each event. You can also keep only
events with nothing else near them, or compare the groups with an earlier
period.

### Show the events

With the lens **Around an event**, **Show** sets how the events are shown:

| Show | What it shows |
|------|---------------|
| **Over time** | The signals from before to after the events. |
| **Each event** | Each event as one point: one figure (for example, the peak rise) against another (for example, the glucose at the event). A line can go through the points. |
| **Spread** | How many events had each value of one figure. |
| **When** | How many events started at each hour of the day, or on each weekday. |

An earlier period shows only with **Over time**. If you change to another way,
the earlier period is removed, and a line under **Show** tells you so.

### Groups of days

With the lenses **Typical day** and **Day by day**, you can compare groups of
days:

- **Weekday**: for example, Monday to Friday against Saturday and Sunday.
- **What happened**: the days with a low, with exercise, with illness, or with a
  total of carbs that you set, against all the other days.

Each group of what happened shows how many days it has. A day that is only partly
in the range is in neither group.

## Add blocks

A report can have more than one block. Under the last block, select:

- **Add a chart** for one more chart.
- **Add figures** for key figures, such as time in range.
- **Add a table** for one row for each treatment, day, event or reading.
- **Add a comparison** for two periods side by side.

Each block can have its own range. When a report is at a limit, the add buttons
are not available, and a line tells you the limit.

## Save and open a report

Select **Save**, type a name, and select **Save the report**. Sometimes you
cannot save the report, for example when a chart has no signals. Then **Save**
is not available, and the reason shows next to it.

To change a saved report, open it and select **Edit**.

- A report that you made with a question opens in the same builder, with its
  signals, lens and groups.
- A report that was not made in this builder, or that was changed outside it,
  opens in the form. A line at the top tells you so.

## The form

The form shows each setting of a report: its panels, tracks and layers, and the
data each one reads.

To move a report from the builder to the form, open **All settings** at the
bottom of the report and select **Change in the form**. After you save a report
from the form, it opens only in the form.

## Good to know

- **Other members' reports.** You can change or delete only the reports that you
  made, unless you can change all reports. To keep your own version of another
  member's report, select **Save as**.
- **The last 24 hours only.** If your access is limited to the last 24 hours,
  you cannot make, change or copy reports.
- **Only the data you can read.** A report shows only the data that your access
  includes. A part that needs other data says which data it needs.

<Callout type="warning" title="A pattern, not a reason">

  A custom report shows what happened. It does not show why, and it does not
  tell you what to change. A group with few events can show a pattern that is
  not there: each group shows how many events or days it has. Meals, activity,
  sleep, illness and sensor problems can all change the figures. A custom report
  is not medical advice. Discuss a pattern with your diabetes care team before
  you change your treatment.

</Callout>
